{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_13","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -B -c 'import pathlib,sqlite3,json,re\nroot=pathlib.Path('\\\\''poc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/run'\\\\'').resolve()\ndef conn(run,db):\n c=sqlite3.connect((root/f'\\\\''old-{run}/prod/{db}.sqlite'\\\\'').as_uri()+'\\\\''?mode=ro'\\\\'',uri=True)\n c.row_factory=sqlite3.Row\n return c\nfor i,call,select in [(2,8729,'\\\\''e5056|e3943|e4762'\\\\''),(2,8737,'\\\\''e3219|e976|e3222'\\\\''),(2,8734,'\\\\''e2620|e2889|e3474|e3934|e4648|e1732'\\\\''),(2,8595,'\\\\''e1786|e2535|e1926'\\\\''),(3,9166,'\\\\''e3660|e4856|e5399'\\\\'')]:\n a=conn(i,'\\\\''archive'\\\\''); v=conn(i,'\\\\''views'\\\\'')\n rr=a.execute('\\\\''select request_sha256,request_json,answer_json from model_calls where call_id=?'\\\\'',(call,)).fetchone()\n j=v.execute('\\\\''select request_sha256,view_id,publication_day from judgements where call_id=?'\\\\'',(call,)).fetchone()\n assert rr[0]==j[0]\n request=json.loads(rr[1])['\\\\''messages'\\\\''][-1]['\\\\''content'\\\\'']\n ans=json.loads(rr[2])\n print('\\\\''DRAW'\\\\'',i,call,j['\\\\''view_id'\\\\''],j['\\\\''publication_day'\\\\''],ans['\\\\''rung'\\\\''])\n for field in ('\\\\''reference'\\\\'','\\\\''implied_outcome'\\\\'','\\\\''reasons'\\\\'','\\\\''cites'\\\\''): print(field,ans[field])\n for m in re.finditer(rf'\\\\''\"'^- (?:{select}) '\"\\\\[.*\\\\n    Quote:.*\"'$'\"'\\\\'',request,re.M): print(m[0])\n print()\np=pathlib.Path('\\\\''docs/research/2026-11-01-e2e-loop/judgement-gold'\\\\'')\nfor e in json.loads((p/'\\\\''gold-v1.json'\\\\'').read_text())['\\\\''entries'\\\\'']:\n if e['\\\\''question_id'\\\\''] in ('\\\\''q01'\\\\'','\\\\''q02'\\\\'','\\\\''q19'\\\\'','\\\\''q22'\\\\''):print(e['\\\\''question_id'\\\\''],e['\\\\''reference'\\\\'']['\\\\''text'\\\\''],e['\\\\''implied_outcome'\\\\'']['\\\\''text'\\\\''],e['\\\\''against'\\\\'']['\\\\''text'\\\\''])\n'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_13","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -B -c 'import pathlib,sqlite3,json,re\nroot=pathlib.Path('\\\\''poc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/run'\\\\'').resolve()\ndef conn(run,db):\n c=sqlite3.connect((root/f'\\\\''old-{run}/prod/{db}.sqlite'\\\\'').as_uri()+'\\\\''?mode=ro'\\\\'',uri=True)\n c.row_factory=sqlite3.Row\n return c\nfor i,call,select in [(2,8729,'\\\\''e5056|e3943|e4762'\\\\''),(2,8737,'\\\\''e3219|e976|e3222'\\\\''),(2,8734,'\\\\''e2620|e2889|e3474|e3934|e4648|e1732'\\\\''),(2,8595,'\\\\''e1786|e2535|e1926'\\\\''),(3,9166,'\\\\''e3660|e4856|e5399'\\\\'')]:\n a=conn(i,'\\\\''archive'\\\\''); v=conn(i,'\\\\''views'\\\\'')\n rr=a.execute('\\\\''select request_sha256,request_json,answer_json from model_calls where call_id=?'\\\\'',(call,)).fetchone()\n j=v.execute('\\\\''select request_sha256,view_id,publication_day from judgements where call_id=?'\\\\'',(call,)).fetchone()\n assert rr[0]==j[0]\n request=json.loads(rr[1])['\\\\''messages'\\\\''][-1]['\\\\''content'\\\\'']\n ans=json.loads(rr[2])\n print('\\\\''DRAW'\\\\'',i,call,j['\\\\''view_id'\\\\''],j['\\\\''publication_day'\\\\''],ans['\\\\''rung'\\\\''])\n for field in ('\\\\''reference'\\\\'','\\\\''implied_outcome'\\\\'','\\\\''reasons'\\\\'','\\\\''cites'\\\\''): print(field,ans[field])\n for m in re.finditer(rf'\\\\''\"'^- (?:{select}) '\"\\\\[.*\\\\n    Quote:.*\"'$'\"'\\\\'',request,re.M): print(m[0])\n print()\np=pathlib.Path('\\\\''docs/research/2026-11-01-e2e-loop/judgement-gold'\\\\'')\nfor e in json.loads((p/'\\\\''gold-v1.json'\\\\'').read_text())['\\\\''entries'\\\\'']:\n if e['\\\\''question_id'\\\\''] in ('\\\\''q01'\\\\'','\\\\''q02'\\\\'','\\\\''q19'\\\\'','\\\\''q22'\\\\''):print(e['\\\\''question_id'\\\\''],e['\\\\''reference'\\\\'']['\\\\''text'\\\\''],e['\\\\''implied_outcome'\\\\'']['\\\\''text'\\\\''],e['\\\\''against'\\\\'']['\\\\''text'\\\\''])\n'\"","aggregated_output":"DRAW 2 8729 v14 2026-09-20 2\nreference Market expectation is about $411 billion for FY2027, rung 2 ($400 billion to less than $450 billion). Zacks' 87.4% growth estimate applied to FY2026 revenue of approximately $215.9 billion implies about $405 billion, also rung 2.\nimplied_outcome About $419 billion: Q1 $81.6 billion plus Q2 $96.2 billion, estimated Q3 of about $113.5 billion (the $110.16 billion guidance high end plus the reported average 3.1% beat), and Q4 of about $127 billion by carrying forward the guided 12% sequential growth.\nreasons The reference is about $411 billion for FY2027, which sits at rung 2; the cited Zacks growth estimate also implies about $405 billion using FY2026 revenue of $215.9 billion. The operating run-rate implies about $419 billion: estimate Q3 at roughly $113.5 billion by applying the cited average 3.1% beat to the $110.16 billion high end of guidance, then carry the guided 12% sequential growth forward to Q4, about $127 billion, alongside reported Q1 and Q2 revenue. Supply constraints are a meaningful downside risk, but the cited estimate of more than $100 billion in unfulfilled demand is for “next year” and is not quantified as a reduction to FY2027 revenue. The implied outcome remains above $400 billion and below $450 billion, so it stays at rung 2.\ncites ['e5056', 'e4702', 'e4377', 'e238', 'e2497', 'e2499', 'e3943', 'e4761', 'e4762', 'e5073', 'e4769', 'e4901']\n- e3943 [party_statement; The Motley Fool author; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:89698c9955cf8aa35b642970 2026-09-04] NVIDIA's FY2027 Q3 guidance implied total revenue growth of 12% sequentially at the midpoint.\n    Quote: In the meantime, Nvidia's fiscal 2027 third-quarter guidance implies 12% sequential growth at the midpoint.\n- e4762 [third_party_view; Manali Pradhan, CFA; original source Manali Pradhan, CFA 2026-09-15; from gnews:9fd93b942948c5002e13eb0b 2026-09-15] Manali Pradhan said NVIDIA exceeded the high end of revenue guidance in each of the past 12 quarters, averaging 3.1% above the high end over the past four quarters.\n    Quote: Nvidia has exceeded the high end of its revenue guidance in each of the past 12 quarters. Over the past four quarters, actual revenue has averaged 3.1% above the high end of guidance.\n- e5056 [third_party_view; supercarblondie.com; original source Wall Street analysts ; from gnews:01edb4d1fa96705133b29207 2026-09-19] Wall Street analysts expect NVIDIA to generate around $411 billion of revenue in FY2027.\n    Quote: Wall Street analysts expect Nvidia to generate around $411 billion in revenue in fiscal 2027.\n\nDRAW 2 8737 v9 2026-09-20 2\nreference The market reference supplied by the previous conclusion is approximately $414 billion for FY2027, which sits at rung 2 ($400 billion to below $450 billion).\nimplied_outcome Approximately $410 billion: $164 billion reported in the first half, about $100 billion implied for Q3 from the $108 billion total-revenue guide and recent Data Center mix, and roughly $146 billion estimated for Q4. The Q4 estimate reflects management's expected hyperscale reacceleration and Rubin ramp, with a discounted contribution from SemiAnalysis's forecast that second-half Data Center revenue would exceed analyst estimates by 20%.\nreasons The market reference is approximately $414 billion for FY2027, which is rung 2. The evidence-based estimate is now around $410 billion: NVIDIA reported $164 billion in the first half, Q3 total-revenue guidance and the recent Data Center mix imply roughly $100 billion for Q3, and a roughly $146 billion Q4 is supported by the expected hyperscale reacceleration and Rubin ramp. SemiAnalysis's forecast of second-half Data Center revenue 20% above analyst estimates is a meaningful upside input: applying it to the roughly $250 billion of second-half revenue implied by the $414 billion reference would point to about $464 billion for the year. I discount that forecast because it does not state its underlying estimate and its implied Q4 is much higher than the company has quantified; the resulting central estimate remains near $410 billion. That crosses the $400 billion boundary into rung 2, but not the $450 billion boundary. Supply and competition risks, and NVIDIA's exclusion of China compute revenue from its outlook, temper the upside.\ncites ['e74', 'e2517', 'e2574', 'e4995', 'e2848', 'e3219', 'e2567', 'e3222', 'e4765', 'e976', 'e2510', 'e4556', 'e4557', 'e2562', 'e2572', 'e109', 'e153', 'e4894', 'e4996']\n- e976 [third_party_view; fool.com author Lyle Daly; original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:bdd09874d53aef7b27866d44 2026-07-26] SemiAnalysis forecast that NVIDIA Data Center revenue in the second half of FY2027 would exceed analyst estimates by 20%.\n    Quote: And even though SemiAnalysis reported the delays, it's still bullish on Nvidia, forecasting that the chipmaker's data center revenue will exceed analyst estimates by 20% in the second half of its fiscal 2027.\n- e3219 [third_party_view; fool.com (Daniel Sparks); original source fool.com (Daniel Sparks) 2026-08-28; from gnews:387b486b6e29491b9c8506df 2026-08-28] Daniel Sparks calculated that FY2027 Q3 Data Center revenue would be $99.9 billion if its share of total revenue stayed at FY2027 Q2's 92.5%.\n    Quote: Hold the data center mix at last quarter's 92.5%, and data center revenue works out to $99.9 billion -- about $100 million short of the line.\n- e3222 [party_statement; fool.com (Daniel Sparks); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:387b486b6e29491b9c8506df 2026-08-28] NVIDIA said hyperscale-business growth should reaccelerate in FY2027 Q4 as Vera Rubin supply builds.\n    Quote: And the company said growth in its hyperscale business (sales to the giant cloud computing providers and the biggest consumer internet companies) should reaccelerate in the fiscal fourth quarter as Vera Rubin supply builds.\n\nDRAW 2 8734 v29 2026-09-20 3\nreference Bank of America's roughly $70 billion estimate of NVIDIA's equity investments in ecosystem partners; this is rung 3 ($50 billion to below $75 billion). Its period and whether it measures outstanding commitments are unclear.\nimplied_outcome About $53 billion: $25 billion of commitments reported as of July 26 plus $18 billion committed for the remainder of FY2027, then the $3.5 billion MediaTek investment announced after that remainder-of-year figure and a risk-adjusted contribution from the $10 billion Anthropic and $2.5 billion Thinking Machines discussions.\nreasons The reference is Bank of America's roughly $70 billion estimate of NVIDIA's equity investments in ecosystem partners; that sits in rung 3 ($50 billion to below $75 billion), though its period and relation to outstanding commitments are unclear. The disclosed $25 billion commitment balance plus $18 billion committed for the remainder of FY2027 gives a $43 billion base. Adding the $3.5 billion MediaTek investment announced after that remainder-of-year figure, and risk-adjusting the $10 billion Anthropic and $2.5 billion Thinking Machines discussions, implies about $53 billion. The newly reported more than $42 billion of equity stakes purchased in the first half of FY2027 confirms an exceptional pace, but it is spending rather than an additional outstanding commitment balance, so I do not add it again. The $30 billion OpenAI investment was announced before the July 26 commitment snapshot and is not counted again. The implied figure remains in rung 3, below the $75 billion boundary for rung 4.\ncites ['e1732', 'e1919', 'e2080', 'e2207', 'e2404', 'e2620', 'e2643', 'e2889', 'e3474', 'e3880', 'e3934', 'e4486', 'e4487', 'e4648', 'e4669', 'e4729', 'e5011']\n- e1732 [third_party_view; finance.yahoo.com; original source Bank of America analyst Vivek Arya ; from gnews:d024d71d75661d369798d307 2026-08-12] Bank of America analyst Vivek Arya said NVIDIA had committed to provide $70 billion in equity investments to ecosystem partners; the period covered was not specified.\n    Quote: Nvidia has committed to directly providing $70 billion in equity investments into ecosystem partners, per new research from Bank of America analyst Vivek Arya.\n- e2620 [fact; NVIDIA Corporation; original source NVIDIA Corporation 2026-08-26; from edgar:NVDA:0001045810-26-000075:nvda-20260726.htm 2026-08-26] NVIDIA reported $99 billion of equity investments and $25 billion of equity investment commitments as of 2026-07-26.\n    Quote: These include equity investments of $99 billion and equity investment commitments of $25 billion as of July 26, 2026.\n- e2889 [party_statement; Business Insider, reporting NVIDIA's statement; original source NVIDIA 2026-08-26; from gnews:aec1fae1b903de5c43ba7fdf 2026-08-26] NVIDIA said on Wednesday that it has $18 billion committed to equity investments for the remainder of its fiscal year, in addition to $47.9 billion held in private companies.\n    Quote: The company said Wednesday that it has $18 billion committed to equity investments for the rest of its fiscal year, on top of $47.9 billion it already holds in private companies.\n- e3474 [fact; insidermonkey.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:baa07a74334dc91adc7d85bc 2026-08-31] NVIDIA is investing $3.5 billion in MediaTek through convertible bonds.\n    Quote: NVIDIA Corporation (NASDAQ: NVDA ) is investing $3.5 billion in Taiwan-based MediaTek through convertible bonds, taking an existing AI partnership a step further.\n- e3934 [rumour; finance.yahoo.com; original source finance.yahoo.com ; from gnews:67a905d722fae62095fd741e 2026-09-04] NVIDIA is reportedly discussing an approximately $2.5 billion investment in Thinking Machines Lab; no completion date is stated.\n    Quote: Nvidia is separately in discussions to invest approximately $2.5 billion\n- e4648 [rumour; Calcalist Tech, citing Reuters; original source Unnamed people familiar with the discussions cited by Reuters ; from gnews:767d795fbdf40bc90ce11e15 2026-09-14] People familiar with discussions told Reuters that Nvidia could invest up to $10 billion in Anthropic’s potential IPO.\n    Quote: Nvidia could invest as much as $10 billion\n\nDRAW 2 8595 v6 2026-09-19 4\nreference Calendar-2027 hyperscaler spending consensus is about $1.08 trillion (e1926), in rung 3 ($900 billion to below $1.1 trillion).\nimplied_outcome About $1.19 trillion for the four named hyperscalers: apply the prior approximate $730 billion/$800 billion four-to-five share proxy to NVIDIA’s $1.3 trillion top-five calendar-2027 CapEx estimate (e2535; repeated in e4756 and later reports).\nreasons The market reference is approximately $1.08 trillion for calendar 2027, which is rung 3. The implied estimate remains about $1.19 trillion for the four named hyperscalers: apply the prior approximate four-to-five share proxy (about $730 billion of $800 billion) to NVIDIA’s $1.3 trillion top-five estimate. That is above rung 4’s $1.1 trillion floor but below rung 5’s $1.2 trillion floor. New grid-connection and other execution constraints increase downside risk, but the evidence does not quantify a reduction sufficient to cross either boundary; repeated reports of the $1.3 trillion estimate do not independently raise it. The share proxy is approximate because the underlying figures do not have perfectly matched scope.\ncites ['e1926', 'e2535', 'e3391', 'e3645', 'e3646', 'e4756', 'e4789', 'e4857', 'e4990']\n- e1786 [third_party_view; Patrick Sanders, The Motley Fool; original source Amazon, Meta Platforms, Microsoft, and Alphabet ; from gnews:c8ac167e2356a75994ebc26f 2026-08-13] Amazon, Meta Platforms, Microsoft, and Alphabet indicated they expect combined AI-infrastructure spending to exceed $730 billion in calendar 2026 and increase in calendar 2027.\n    Quote: Amazon , Meta Platforms , Microsoft , and Alphabet have indicated they will spend more than $730 billion combined on AI infrastructure this year, and they expect that number to increase next year.\n- e1926 [third_party_view; uk.finance.yahoo.com, citing Bank of America; original source Bank of America ; from gnews:1219126db2e6631aa1264142 2026-08-16] Bank of America said analyst consensus for hyperscalers’ calendar 2027 spending rose from $480 billion in August 2025 to $1.08 trillion in August 2026, an increase of about 127%.\n    Quote: What analysts expect the hyperscalers to spend in 2027 alone has more than doubled in the space of a year, from a consensus of $480 billion (€416bn) in August 2025 to $1.08 trillion (€943bn) this month, a rise of about 127%, according to Bank of America.\n- e2535 [party_statement; Colette Kress; original source NVIDIA 2026-08-26; from truenorth:transcript:NVDA:2026-08-26 2026-08-26] NVIDIA said cloud-industry backlog exceeded $2 trillion and top-five hyperscaler capital expenditures were expected to approach $800 billion in 2026 and $1.3 trillion in 2027.\n    Quote: With cloud industry backlog now greater than $2 trillion, CapEx by the top 5 hyperscalers is expected to reach nearly $800 billion in 2026 and $1.3 trillion in 2027.\n\nDRAW 3 9166 v41 2026-09-20 3\nreference NVIDIA management’s stated expectation that FY2028 CPU revenue will more than double the $20 billion FY2027 expectation implies more than $40 billion, which is rung 2.\nimplied_outcome Roughly $38–39 billion for FY2028: the stated more-than-double expectation points above $40 billion, but is discounted for a forward Vera target without shipment volume in hand, with the competitive evidence adding execution risk.\nreasons The reference is NVIDIA management’s FY2028 expectation of more than double its $20 billion FY2027 CPU-revenue expectation, implying more than $40 billion and placing the reference in rung 2. I discount that forward target to roughly $38–39 billion: the evidence shows broad intended deployment and an Anthropic evaluation, but not Vera shipment volume in hand or a sized customer commitment; AMD’s reported performance comparisons add execution and competitiveness risk. That estimate remains below the $40 billion rung-2 boundary.\ncites ['e3660', 'e4687', 'e4689', 'e4691', 'e4692', 'e4764', 'e4856', 'e4857', 'e5044', 'e5399', 'e5400', 'e5401', 'e5402', 'e5403', 'e5404']\n- e3660 [party_statement; Bram Berkowitz, fool.com, reporting Colette Kress's statement; original source NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress ; from gnews:fd25a8ea6f60290264bfbe55 2026-08-30] NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress said NVIDIA still expects $20 billion of CPU revenue in FY2027 and expects FY2028 CPU revenue to more than double that amount.\n    Quote: The bad news for AMD and Intel is that Kress said Nvidia not only still expects $20 billion in CPU revenue in its fiscal 2027, but expects that number to more than double in fiscal 2028.\n- e4856 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA management ; from gnews:c70bc12967b9b53426b5fb10 2026-09-12] NVIDIA management said Vera CPU revenue in FY2028 could be more than double the $20 billion potential it cited for FY2027.\n    Quote: with the potential to more than double that number in fiscal year 2028.\n- e5399 [party_statement; wccftech.com; original source AMD ; from gnews:83ffa482f76b03da59ac2674 2026-09-19] AMD reported that its 6th Gen EPYC Venice CPUs delivered 20% higher per-core performance than NVIDIA’s 88-core Vera CPU in SPEC 2026.\n    Quote: Here, the AMD 6th Gen EPYC Venice CPUs provide a 20% uplift over NVIDIA's 88-Core Vera CPU.\n\nq01 Wall Street consensus $392bn for this fiscal year (i037), consistent with the 82% growth analysts project for this year (i090, i174). Q1 actual $81.6bn (i280) plus Q2 guidance $91bn plus or minus 2% (i281) give $172.6bn for the first half, so consensus needs about $219bn in the second half. Management says growth rates are accelerating as quarterly revenue approaches $100bn (i295); SemiAnalysis tracks second-half data-center compute 20% above consensus and shipment volumes materially above market estimates (i106, i208, i111); Rubin first shipments are scheduled for Q3 2026 with a volume ramp in Q4 (i219, i105) at about double the GB300 rack price (i216); management targets $20bn of Vera CPU sales by fiscal year-end (i288). A second half of $228-262bn gives $400-435bn. Unnamed reports of delays across the upcoming Vera Rubin server lineup (i133); the Kyber report leaves 2026 Rubin on the Oberon rack untouched (i202), but memory stays tight until 2028 (i252) and the guidance assumes no China data-center compute revenue (i281).\nq02 No segment consensus is stated; consensus total revenue of $392bn (i037) at the Q1 Data Center share of 92% of sales (i129) implies about $360bn. Q1 Data Center $75.25bn (i129, i151); Q2 total guided $91bn (i281) at the same share is about $84bn, so the first half is roughly $159bn. My total-revenue view of $400-435bn (q01: i295, i106, i219) at a 92-93% share, with SemiAnalysis expecting second-half data-center compute 20% above consensus (i106, i208), gives $370-400bn. Unnamed reports of delays across the Vera Rubin server lineup (i133); the guidance assumes zero China data-center compute revenue (i281, i156).\nq19 Management's own figure: $20bn of stand-alone CPU sales this year (i095), by the end of this fiscal year (i288), nearly $20bn with no period (i080): one claim retold three times. The first stand-alone CPU launches this fall (i096), so the revenue falls in roughly two quarters; at Morgan Stanley's $5,000 per Vera CPU (i218), $20bn is four million units. Demand signals are real: Perplexity plans to deploy Vera in production (i262, i291) and Intel's CEO sees the CPU:GPU ratio in inference moving toward 1:1 (i009); but NVIDIA chose Intel Xeon 6 as host CPU for DGX Rubin NVL8 (i008). I bet $8-18bn, below the $20bn ambition. Management expects $20bn by fiscal year-end (i288, i095), and Intel's CEO describes inference moving toward one CPU per GPU (i009).\nq22 Wall Street consensus $554bn (i037), the 41% growth analysts project for next year (i090, i174). A higher FY2027 base of $400-435bn (q01: i295, i106) at the Street's 41% (i090) is $565-615bn; NVIDIA projects the big four's capex rising from $650bn to over $1 trillion in 2027 (i256, i173), a 54% step; Huang's $1 trillion of Blackwell and Rubin sales through 2027 (i079) exceeds consensus total revenue over the window; Rubin racks sell at about twice the GB300 price (i216) and Vera CPUs add a line (i288). Against: the Kyber rack may slip to 2028 (i100) with Oberon-Rubin racks filling the gap (i181), and memory stays tight until 2028 (i252). I bet $560-640bn. SemiAnalysis's Kyber delay to 2028 with the four-die Rubin Ultra cancelled (i100, i179) pushes the next product step out of FY2028, and Meta's CEO sees slower AI-agent progress (i292).\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_14","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -B -c 'import pathlib,sys,inspect\nfolder=pathlib.Path('\\\\''docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers'\\\\'').resolve()\nsys.path.insert(0,str(folder))\nimport gate as g, calc_check as c\nsource=inspect.getsource(c._source_errors)\nnamespace=vars(c).copy()\nexec(source.replace(\\\"e['\\\\''text'\\\\''] == fig.get('\\\\''expression'\\\\'')\\\", \\\"fig.get('\\\\''expression'\\\\'') in e['\\\\''text'\\\\'']\\\"),namespace)\nplant=g.src('\\\\''t'\\\\'','\\\\''e2535'\\\\'','\\\\''\"'$800 billion'\"'\\\\'',800.0,'\\\\''USD bn'\\\\'','\\\\''guidance'\\\\'',g.W('\\\\''five'\\\\'','\\\\''capex'\\\\'','\\\\''combined'\\\\'','\\\\''CY2026'\\\\''),'\\\\''bound'\\\\'','\\\\''approx_below'\\\\'')\nprint('\\\\''exact plant'\\\\'',c._source_errors(plant,g.R3_PASSAGES));print('\\\\''substring mutant plant'\\\\'',namespace['\\\\''_source_errors'\\\\''](plant,g.R3_PASSAGES))\nf3,d3=g.g3()\nd3['\\\\''figures'\\\\''].append(g.comp('\\\\''reverse'\\\\'','\\\\''product'\\\\'',['\\\\''gunits'\\\\'','\\\\''gasp'\\\\''],20.,'\\\\''USD bn'\\\\'',g.W('\\\\''NVIDIA'\\\\'','\\\\''revenue'\\\\'','\\\\''Vera CPU'\\\\'','\\\\''FY2027'\\\\'')))\ntry:\n print('\\\\''reverse checked'\\\\'',c.check(f3,g.G_PASSAGES,d3))\nexcept KeyError as e: print('\\\\''registered reverse product raises'\\\\'',repr(e))\nf4,d4=g.g4()\nres=c.check(f4,g.G_PASSAGES,d4)\nfig={f['\\\\''id'\\\\'']:f for f in d4['\\\\''figures'\\\\'']}\nprint('\\\\''G4 original shared declaration'\\\\'',f4['\\\\''shared'\\\\''],'\\\\''nodes'\\\\'',fig['\\\\''gfy'\\\\'']['\\\\''low'\\\\''],fig['\\\\''gfy'\\\\'']['\\\\''high'\\\\''],'\\\\''result'\\\\'',res)\nd4['\\\\''answers'\\\\''][1]['\\\\''rung'\\\\'']=3\nprint('\\\\''range rung controls'\\\\'',c.check(f4,g.G_PASSAGES,d4))\n'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_14","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -B -c 'import pathlib,sys,inspect\nfolder=pathlib.Path('\\\\''docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers'\\\\'').resolve()\nsys.path.insert(0,str(folder))\nimport gate as g, calc_check as c\nsource=inspect.getsource(c._source_errors)\nnamespace=vars(c).copy()\nexec(source.replace(\\\"e['\\\\''text'\\\\''] == fig.get('\\\\''expression'\\\\'')\\\", \\\"fig.get('\\\\''expression'\\\\'') in e['\\\\''text'\\\\'']\\\"),namespace)\nplant=g.src('\\\\''t'\\\\'','\\\\''e2535'\\\\'','\\\\''\"'$800 billion'\"'\\\\'',800.0,'\\\\''USD bn'\\\\'','\\\\''guidance'\\\\'',g.W('\\\\''five'\\\\'','\\\\''capex'\\\\'','\\\\''combined'\\\\'','\\\\''CY2026'\\\\''),'\\\\''bound'\\\\'','\\\\''approx_below'\\\\'')\nprint('\\\\''exact plant'\\\\'',c._source_errors(plant,g.R3_PASSAGES));print('\\\\''substring mutant plant'\\\\'',namespace['\\\\''_source_errors'\\\\''](plant,g.R3_PASSAGES))\nf3,d3=g.g3()\nd3['\\\\''figures'\\\\''].append(g.comp('\\\\''reverse'\\\\'','\\\\''product'\\\\'',['\\\\''gunits'\\\\'','\\\\''gasp'\\\\''],20.,'\\\\''USD bn'\\\\'',g.W('\\\\''NVIDIA'\\\\'','\\\\''revenue'\\\\'','\\\\''Vera CPU'\\\\'','\\\\''FY2027'\\\\'')))\ntry:\n print('\\\\''reverse checked'\\\\'',c.check(f3,g.G_PASSAGES,d3))\nexcept KeyError as e: print('\\\\''registered reverse product raises'\\\\'',repr(e))\nf4,d4=g.g4()\nres=c.check(f4,g.G_PASSAGES,d4)\nfig={f['\\\\''id'\\\\'']:f for f in d4['\\\\''figures'\\\\'']}\nprint('\\\\''G4 original shared declaration'\\\\'',f4['\\\\''shared'\\\\''],'\\\\''nodes'\\\\'',fig['\\\\''gfy'\\\\'']['\\\\''low'\\\\''],fig['\\\\''gfy'\\\\'']['\\\\''high'\\\\''],'\\\\''result'\\\\'',res)\nd4['\\\\''answers'\\\\''][1]['\\\\''rung'\\\\'']=3\nprint('\\\\''range rung controls'\\\\'',c.check(f4,g.G_PASSAGES,d4))\n'\"","aggregated_output":"exact plant [('C1', 't: \"$800 billion\" is not a complete numeric expression of e2535')]\nsubstring mutant plant []\nregistered reverse product raises KeyError('unit')\nG4 original shared declaration [] nodes 400.0 435.0 result {'errors': [], 'recorded': []}\nrange rung controls {'errors': [('C6', 'v22: rung 3 ([480, 550]) contains no part of its outcome 560.0–640.0')], 'recorded': []}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_15","type":"agent_message","text":"I reproduced the 29/29 output. I also found two cases it misses: R4 still reports a definite crossing after I reverse the comparison bound, and G4 still passes after I replace q01’s forecast with the $392B consensus."}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_16","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n 'Before writing a test|Mutate|first failure|failed step|Before any paid run|never change the|unit.*size|The prompt audit|A prompt serves' AGENTS.md; sed -n '130,162p' docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/DESIGN.md\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_16","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n 'Before writing a test|Mutate|first failure|failed step|Before any paid run|never change the|unit.*size|The prompt audit|A prompt serves' AGENTS.md; sed -n '130,162p' docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/DESIGN.md\"","aggregated_output":"149:- Before writing a test, read every function its fixture drives: return shapes, failure classification, the data and attributes it\n155:- Write the one decisive assertion that fails without the code under test, and predict the run before running it. A first failure for\n158:- Mutate on a scratch copy: the test must fail on the mutant while the unmutated control passes. An error is not a catch until its\n182:- Before any paid run, `python3 scripts/preflight.py` replays the stored model answers and matching records of the archives and ledgers\n200:- **The prompt audit runs after every measurement and before any prompt change** (`.claude/skills/prompt-audit/SKILL.md`). The whole\n214:- **A prompt serves every company, never the bench.** Every analyst prompt is a general hedge-fund brain: each rule and example reads the\n375:  A failed step of a plan is fixed and re-run at that same step: its failures are the work list, and nothing later in the plan and no\n\n## 5. The v13 request text (written and audited at $0 before the gate closes) [46.6]\n\n- **The basis:** judging-v7's standard sentences are kept, among them the reference, run-rate, discount, sizing, identities and\n  consistency.\n- **Replaced where they conflict with a family:**\n  - the single-question delivery (\"the current conclusions of dependencies\");\n  - \"the previous conclusion carries\" pieces not shown, which becomes \"our earlier forecast, not a source\";\n  - the one-reference, one-outcome output sentence;\n  - the dependency-consistency sentence, which becomes the family's own consistency, the same clause made family-wide.\n- **Added:** how to write figures (kinds, bases, expressions, relations, bridges, `adjusts`, `unresolved`).\n- **The audit:** every changed sentence is shown with its source (D260, D263, D725, this design), read against a stored family\n  request, and its character count before and after is reported (AGENTS §6).\n\n## 6. The $0 gate (registered; each defect must produce its named finding) [46.5]\n\n| Item | Content | Pass: the named finding |\n|---|---|---|\n| R1a | old-2 8729 (v14) and 8737 (v9) on 09-20, as stored: v9 Q4 146, v14 Q4 127 | **C3** on Q4 |\n| R1b | the same, with Q3 as stored: **one** guide node (midpoint 108 from e2499, labelled `guidance`) feeds v14's company Q3 forecast (113.5, beat-adjusted) and, on a second path, v9's Data Center Q3 (108 × 92.5% = 99.9) | **C4** on Q3 company revenue (the bypass path), independent of C3 |\n| R1c (control) | the same guide node (midpoint 108 ±2%, e2499's expression) feeding one shared Q3 forecast node (113.5) that both members read | passes |\n| R2 | old-2 8734 as `roll_forward`: the $25B balance as of July 26 (e2620), plus the $18B committed for the rest of the fiscal year (e2889) as `overlap_unknown` and `disputed`, plus MediaTek and the talks as estimates | passes C8; recorded overlap; the alternate without the $18B (about $35B) decides the $50B boundary (conditional) |\n| R3 | old-2 8595: 730 (e1786, bound \">\") / 800 (e2535, bound \"approx_below\") × 1,300 (e2535, point) | stripped qualifiers: **C1**. Qualifiers kept: passes C1, then **C8** without a bridge naming those operands. With the bridge: recorded, undetermined |\n| R4 | old-3 9166: 20 × (>2) (e3660, \"more than double\") gives >40, trimmed to 38–39 by `adjusts: trim` | recorded adjustment that definitely crosses the $40B boundary (rung 2 is $40–50B, rung 1 $50B+) |\n| G1–G4 (controls) | q01, q02, q19, q22, from `gold-v1.json`'s reasoning (expressions from the packet items). q01's Q1 + Q2 as `time_aggregation`; q02's comparison $392B (consensus) × 92% (reported mix); q22 in one family with q01, reading q01's FY2027 node | all pass, estimates visible; q02's reference passes C5; q19's overlapping range passes C6 with its reason |\n| P1–P3 (plants, on control copies) | P1, an amount absent from its expression; P2, a wrong sum; P3a, a prior forecast as the reference; P3b, the same figure wrapped in an estimate that cites outside evidence | P1 **C1**, P2 **C2**, P3a and P3b **C5**, each for its intended reason |\n| T1–T7 (C1 forms, real passages) | e2499 midpoint 108 ±2% (accepted as `midpoint`); e1786 \">730\"; e2535 approx_below 800 and the point 1,300; e3660 \">×2\"; stripped forms \"$730\", \"800\", \"double\" against those passages; 108.5 against e2499 | T1–T3 parse as stated; the stripped forms and 108.5 refused |\n| U1–U2 (paths) | a member answering `unresolved` while another member's outcome needs its shared quantity; a calculation refused twice | the family held, recorded; the family unresolved, recorded |\n\n- **Stop:** any missing finding, a finding for the wrong reason, or a control the format cannot express.\n- **Then:** Council B on the gate's output. The paid gate's ceiling goes to Alex.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
