{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_18","type":"agent_message","text":"GATE FAIL\n\nI reproduced the **29/29** output. The checker still lets G4 use the wrong FY2027 base and lets R4 claim a definite $40B crossing after the comparison bound is reversed. Those are failures of the registered checks.\n\n1. **The G3 fix is a general identity, and rerunning the same step is valid.** Revenue divided by price per unit gives implied unit volume. q19 already uses that reasoning, and `_per_unit` checks the population and product scope without naming Vera. I reproduced run 1 byte for byte by restoring G3’s old measure label, disabling `_per_unit`, and omitting P4. That accounts for every logged difference. The logs cannot establish whether any other fixture fields changed because they contain the results rather than the serialized fixtures. The fix is also incomplete: I tested its documented inverse, four million units × $5,000, and the checker raised `KeyError('unit')`. It looked for the output unit in `what`, which has no unit field. **Fix I’d accept:** check the units in both directions and verify that the inverse gives **$20B** in the declared output unit. Keep P4 as a scope check and save the fixture manifests for both runs. [Checker](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/calc_check.py:372), [Run 1](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/gate-run1.txt), [Run 2](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/gate-run2.txt)\n\n2. **The surviving substring mutant exposes a missing test.** The log shows five of six single mutants caught, plus the combined mutant. The existing stripped-expression cases also strip the qualifier, so qualifier equality can reject them even with exact expression matching disabled. I tested a case from `e2535` that keeps **800**, **USD bn**, and **`approx_below`**, but supplies only `$800 billion` as the expression. The current checker refuses it. A literal substring variant accepts it. **Fix I’d accept:** add that case and require C1 to refuse it specifically because the expression is incomplete. The combined mutant can stay as a separate result. [Mutant log](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/mutants.log), [C1 checker](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/calc_check.py:76)\n\n3. **The reconstructions preserve the main calculations, but the gate doesn’t prove they preserve the decisive reasoning.** I reread calls **8729, 8737, 8734, 8595, and 9166** and verified that the fixture passages match what those requests showed. R1 preserves the separate Q3 bases and the $146B/$127B conflict, but its $414B prior pointer names September 19, when v9 forecast **$385–390B**. The $414B was v9’s own forecast on September 14, call **7939**. R2 preserves the disputed $18B addition, but its $6.5B from talks is inferred from the rounded total. R2 and R3 also replace the stored $70B and $1.08T comparisons with `unavailable`. Those are sensible repairs to test separately. R4 faithfully records the original **above $40B → $38–39B** logic. G1–G4 retain the main numerical relationships, but the gate checks only for an empty error list, with placeholder reasons and empty citations.\n\n   Two read-only mutations show why that matters. **G4** still passed when FY2028 growth used the **$392B consensus** instead of q01’s **$400–435B forecast**. **R4** still passed when I changed the derived bound from `>40` to `<40`. **Fix I’d accept:** bind G4 to q01’s actual forecast node, authenticate prior figures against the dated answer supplied, and propagate bound direction through the arithmetic and sensitivity check. Preserve the original comparisons and their uncertainty in the trace reconstructions. Put deliberate repairs in separately labelled controls. [Fixtures and assertions](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/gate.py:92), [Boundary checker](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/calc_check.py:466), [Old-2 archive](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/run/old-2/prod/archive.sqlite), [Old-3 archive](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/run/old-3/prod/archive.sqlite), [Frozen gold](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-01-e2e-loop/judgement-gold/gold-v1.json)\n\n4. **The request audit misses a narrower rule and a direct conflict.** The schema description asks for an estimate whenever an operand differs in scope, period, or measure. That sweeps in valid calculations such as q01’s quarter-to-half addition and q02’s reported mix applied to outside consensus. C5 would refuse q02 if the model followed that instruction and added an estimate to the comparison. The draft also says earlier forecasts are **only for comparison**, while the design permits a dated `prior_forecast` input. Finally, the schema examples reuse figures from the gate cases, and the audit leaves the rendered request and a schema-valid example unexercised. **Fix I’d accept:** make the schema instructions match the operation-specific rules, settle the permitted use of dated prior forecasts in the full request, and use examples from other industries. Render one stored family request and check a conforming answer through the schema and checker before closing the $0 gate. [Request audit](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/REQUEST-AUDIT.md:29), [Draft prompt](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/judging-v13-draft.txt:3), [AGENTS §6](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/AGENTS.md:194)"}}
